let __v_68 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$dict\":[[\"ambiguous\",\"Ambiguous means it can be read two ways and you cannot tell which was meant: 'the meeting is at some point tomorrow' is ambiguous about the time.\"],[\"bittersweet\",\"Bittersweet is happy and sad in the same moment: the last day of a job you loved, or a child's first day at school.\"],[\"cynical\",\"Cynical means expecting the worst of people's reasons - assuming a charity drive is really about publicity. It is different from sceptical, which just wants proof.\"],[\"mundane\",\"Mundane means ordinary and a bit dull: the mundane jobs like the washing up and the school run. It is not an insult to a person.\"],[\"serendipity\",\"Serendipity is a good thing found while looking for something else: finding a twenty in an old coat, or meeting your best friend by taking the wrong bus.\"],[\"procrastinate\",\"To procrastinate is to put off what you meant to do today, usually by doing something easier instead. It is about the delay, not about being lazy.\"],[\"gaslighting\",\"Gaslighting is making someone doubt their own memory of events: 'that never happened, you're imagining it'. The point is to leave them unsure of themselves.\"],[\"condescending\",\"Condescending means talking down to someone, as if they were slower than you: explaining the job to the person who has done it for ten years.\"],[\"patronising\",\"Patronising is the same talking-down, often with false kindness: 'well done you!' to a competent adult. Condescending is the colder word for it.\"],[\"pretentious\",\"Pretentious means claiming more importance or taste than the thing deserves: a menu that calls chips 'triple-cooked potato batons'.\"],[\"no worries\",\"'No worries' means it is fine and nothing is owed - an answer to an apology or a thank you, not a warning. In Australia and New Zealand it is also the everyday reply to 'thank you'.\"],[\"no hard feelings\",\"'No hard feelings' means you are not holding a grudge: 'you got the job and I didn't, but no hard feelings'. It closes a disappointment without pretending it did not sting.\"],[\"no offence\",\"'No offence' is a warning that the next thing is blunt, as in 'no offence, but the kitchen is a mess'. It does not make an insult kind, so only say it if you would say the same thing to their face.\"],[\"all good\",\"'All good' means nothing is wrong and there is nothing to apologise for - the relaxed answer to 'sorry I'm late' or 'thanks'. It is casual, so keep it out of a formal email.\"],[\"fair enough\",\"'Fair enough' means you accept the answer even if you would rather not - it closes a small disagreement rather than starting one.\"],[\"ballpark figure\",\"A ballpark figure is a rough number, close enough to plan with: 'about two thousand, give or take'. Ballpark on its own means roughly right.\"],[\"touch base\",\"To touch base is to check in briefly with someone: 'I'll touch base on Friday about the timetable'. It is a short catch-up, not a meeting.\"],[\"in two minds\",\"To be in two minds is to be unable to choose: 'I'm in two minds about the job'. The American version is 'on the fence'.\"],[\"on the fence\",\"To be on the fence is to have not decided yet: 'the council is still on the fence about the bins'. Sitting on the fence means avoiding the choice.\"],[\"out of the loop\",\"To be out of the loop is to stop being told what is happening: 'I was on leave, so I'm out of the loop on the merger'.\"],[\"red flag\",\"A red flag is a warning sign worth taking seriously: a deposit asked for before a viewing, or a new job with no contract.\"],[\"grey area\",\"A grey area is a case the rules do not clearly cover either way: taking a sick day for a sick child. Grey in Britain, gray in America.\"],[\"catch 22\",\"A catch-22 is a rule you cannot win either way: you need experience to get the job, and you need the job to get the experience.\"],[\"silver lining\",\"A silver lining is the small good thing inside a bad one: the flight was cancelled, but you got an extra day with your family.\"],[\"cold feet\",\"To get cold feet is to lose your nerve just before a big step: the wedding is booked and now you are panicking. It means doubt, not that you are ill.\"],[\"under the bus\",\"To throw someone under the bus is to blame them to save yourself: the manager took the credit and then blamed the team when it failed.\"],[\"hindsight\",\"Hindsight is seeing what you should have done once it is too late: 'in hindsight we should have booked earlier'. Hindsight is always 20/20 means it looks obvious afterwards.\"],[\"cliche\",\"A cliche is a phrase so overused it has stopped meaning anything: 'thinking outside the box', 'at the end of the day'. Clichéd describes the phrase, not you.\"],[\"food for thought\",\"Food for thought is something worth thinking about: 'her talk on sleep gave me food for thought'. Nothing to do with eating.\"],[\"the big picture\",\"The big picture is the whole situation rather than the detail: stepping back to see whether the plan still makes sense.\"],[\"bonjour\",\"Bonjour is French for hello - literally 'good day'. You say it from morning until the evening, and bonsoir (good evening) after that.\"],[\"bonsoir\",\"Bonsoir is French for good evening. It takes over from bonjour once it gets dark, and it works both arriving and leaving.\"],[\"au revoir\",\"Au revoir is French for goodbye - literally 'until we see each other again'. Bonjour when you arrive, au revoir when you go.\"],[\"merci\",\"Merci is French for thank you. Merci beaucoup is thank you very much, and the reply is je vous en prie or, informally, pas de problème.\"],[\"merci beaucoup\",\"Merci beaucoup is French for thank you very much - merci is thanks, beaucoup is a lot.\"],[\"sil vous plait\",\"S'il vous plaît is French for please - literally 'if it pleases you'. Say it when you ask for something, and merci when you get it.\"],[\"bon appetit\",\"Bon appétit is French for enjoy your meal - literally 'good appetite'. Said before eating, the way people say 'enjoy' in English.\"],[\"ciao\",\"Ciao is Italian for hello and goodbye, both - you say it arriving and leaving. Arrivederci is the more formal goodbye.\"],[\"arrivederci\",\"Arrivederci is Italian for goodbye - literally 'until we see each other again'. Ciao does the same job between friends.\"],[\"grazie\",\"Grazie is Italian for thank you. Grazie mille (a thousand thanks) is the warmer version, and prego is the reply - you're welcome.\"],[\"prego\",\"Prego is Italian for you're welcome - the answer to grazie. It also means 'please, go ahead' when someone waits for you to pass.\"],[\"hola\",\"Hola is Spanish for hello. It works all day, and buenos días (good morning), buenas tardes (afternoon) and buenas noches (evening) are the politer versions.\"],[\"adios\",\"Adiós is Spanish for goodbye. Hasta luego (see you later) is the softer way out of a conversation.\"],[\"gracias\",\"Gracias is Spanish for thank you. Muchas gracias is thanks a lot, and de nada is the reply - you're welcome.\"],[\"por favor\",\"Por favor is Spanish for please - literally 'by favour'. Gracias is the thank you that goes with it.\"],[\"danke\",\"Danke is German for thank you. Danke schön is thanks a lot, and bitte is the reply - you're welcome, or please, depending on how it is used.\"],[\"bitte\",\"Bitte is German for please and for you're welcome, depending on the moment: bitte when you ask, bitte when you hand something over. Danke is thanks.\"],[\"auf wiedersehen\",\"Auf Wiedersehen is German for goodbye - literally 'until we see each other again'. Tschüss is the casual version between friends.\"],[\"guten tag\",\"Guten Tag is German for good day - hello, in the middle of the day. Guten Morgen is good morning and Guten Abend good evening.\"],[\"salud\",\"Salud is Spanish for health. It is the toast - the same job as 'cheers' - and also what you say after a sneeze.\"],[\"namaste\",\"Namaste is Hindi for hello and goodbye, said with the palms together. It means roughly 'I bow to you'.\"],[\"sayonara\",\"Sayonara is Japanese for goodbye. Konnichiwa is hello, and arigato is thank you.\"],[\"konnichiwa\",\"Konnichiwa is Japanese for hello - strictly good afternoon, though it works as a general hello. Arigato is thank you.\"],[\"arigato\",\"Arigato is Japanese for thank you. Arigato gozaimasu is the politer, fuller form.\"],[\"ni hao\",\"Nǐ hǎo is Mandarin for hello - literally 'you good'. Xièxie (thank you) is the pair you will use most.\"],[\"buenos dias\",\"Buenos días is Spanish for good morning. Hola does for hello all day, and buenas noches is good evening or good night.\"],[\"buenas noches\",\"Buenas noches is Spanish for good evening, and also good night when you go to bed. Hasta mañana is see you tomorrow.\"],[\"hasta luego\",\"Hasta luego is Spanish for see you later - the everyday goodbye. Adiós is the fuller goodbye, and hasta mañana means see you tomorrow.\"],[\"mucho gusto\",\"Mucho gusto is Spanish for pleased to meet you - literally 'much pleasure'. The reply is igualmente, likewise.\"],[\"de nada\",\"De nada is Spanish for you're welcome - literally 'of nothing'. It answers gracias, the way 'no problem' does here.\"],[\"como estas\",\"Cómo estás is Spanish for how are you. The answer people expect is bien, gracias (well, thanks).\"],[\"que pasa\",\"Qué pasa is Spanish for what's happening - a casual 'what's up'. Qué tal is the softer version.\"],[\"xiexie\",\"Xièxie is Mandarin for thank you. Bù kèqì (you're welcome) is the reply, and nǐ hǎo is hello.\"],[\"oui\",\"Oui is French for yes, and non is no. Oui, merci is yes please, which is the pair you will use most.\"],[\"d accord\",\"D'accord is French for okay or agreed - the sound of 'that's fine'. Say it to agree to a plan.\"],[\"daccord\",\"D'accord is French for okay or agreed - the sound of 'that's fine'. Say it to agree to a plan.\"],[\"ca va\",\"Ça va is French for how are you, and also 'it's fine' as an answer. Ça va bien is 'going well'.\"],[\"pas de probleme\",\"Pas de problème is French for no problem - both 'you're welcome' and 'that's fine'.\"],[\"je vous en prie\",\"Je vous en prie is French for you're welcome, and also 'please do' when you hold a door. It answers merci.\"],[\"tschuss\",\"Tschüss is German for bye - the casual form between friends. Auf Wiedersehen is the formal goodbye.\"],[\"servus\",\"Servus is a greeting in southern Germany and Austria: hello, and also bye, depending on the moment.\"],[\"scheisse\",\"Scheiße is German for shit - the everyday swear word, used far more casually than in English, so hearing it is not as strong as it sounds.\"],[\"molto bene\",\"Molto bene is Italian for very good or very well - molto is very, bene is good.\"]]}")?;
