let __v_69 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$dict\":[[\"gutted\",\"Gutted means deeply disappointed: 'gutted I missed it'. It is the everyday British word for it - stronger than annoyed, milder than devastated.\"],[\"chuffed\",\"Chuffed means pleased: 'chuffed with the result'. Chuffed to bits is very pleased indeed, and not chuffed is put out.\"],[\"knackered\",\"Knackered means exhausted: 'knackered after the move'. It also means broken, as in 'the washing machine is knackered' - the context tells you which.\"],[\"peckish\",\"Peckish means a bit hungry rather than starving: 'I'm peckish' is an invitation to a snack, not to a meal.\"],[\"dodgy\",\"Dodgy means unreliable or not to be trusted: a dodgy knee, dodgy wifi, a dodgy deal. It is a mild word, and it hedges - dodgy suggests you are not certain.\"],[\"cheeky\",\"Cheeky means pleasantly rude or a bit forward: a cheeky joke, a cheeky pint. Brits use it warmly as often as critically, so 'cheeky' is not an insult on its own.\"],[\"gobsmacked\",\"Gobsmacked means so surprised you cannot speak: 'gobsmacked when he turned up'. Gob is slang for mouth, and you are smacked about it.\"],[\"faff\",\"A faff is a job with more fiddly steps than it is worth: 'the paperwork is such a faff'. To faff about is to spend time on small things instead of the real one.\"],[\"kerfuffle\",\"A kerfuffle is a fuss about something small: 'a right kerfuffle over the seating'. It sounds more serious than it is.\"],[\"naff\",\"Naff means unfashionable or a bit cheap: naff souvenirs, a naff joke. It is a gentle dig rather than a real insult.\"],[\"nicked\",\"Nicked means stolen: 'someone nicked my bike'. It is also a narrow win - 'they nicked it 1-0' - and, in police talk, arrested.\"],[\"buzzing\",\"Buzzing means excited and delighted: 'buzzing for the trip'. It is the happy twin of dreading.\"],[\"niggling\",\"Niggling means small but persistent and hard to ignore: a niggling worry, a niggling pain. The size is not the point - the persistence is.\"],[\"miffed\",\"Miffed means mildly annoyed or put out: 'a bit miffed she wasn't asked'. Milder than angry, and usually about something small.\"],[\"cheesed off\",\"Cheesed off means fed up: 'cheesed off with the weather'. Brassed off and browned off mean the same thing.\"],[\"cringe\",\"Cringe is the wince you feel at someone else's embarrassment: 'watching him sing was pure cringe'. As a verb, the memory makes you cringe.\"],[\"salty\",\"Salty means bitter and sulky about losing or being left out: 'still salty about the game'. The food sense is the plain one - too much salt - so the joke is that the person is tasting tears.\"],[\"sus\",\"Sus means suspicious or not quite right, of a thing or a person: 'that email looks sus', 'he was acting sus all night'. It is short for suspicious.\"],[\"no cap\",\"'No cap' means no lie: 'it's the best one, no cap'. Cap is the lie and capping is exaggerating, so no cap is the promise that you mean it.\"],[\"vibes\",\"Vibes are the feeling a place or person gives off: 'good vibes', 'a bad vibe'. Not something you can measure, which is the point of the word.\"],[\"ghosting\",\"Ghosting is disappearing on someone without a word: no reply, no explanation, just silence. It is common enough in dating to have its own word.\"],[\"catfish\",\"To catfish is to invent an online identity to draw someone into a relationship: the person you have been talking to is not who the photos showed.\"],[\"doomscrolling\",\"Doomscrolling is scrolling bad news long after you have stopped taking it in. The feed carries on; the mood does not recover.\"],[\"fomo\",\"FOMO is fear of missing out - the tug you feel seeing everyone else's weekend. It is said as a word, 'fomo', not spelled out.\"],[\"clickbait\",\"Clickbait is a headline written to get the click rather than to tell you anything: 'you won't believe what happened next'.\"],[\"troll\",\"A troll is someone who posts to wind people up rather than to argue in good faith. Answering them is the contact they wanted.\"],[\"meme\",\"A meme is a joke or an idea copied and varied across the internet: one picture with a caption that people keep reworking.\"],[\"empathy\",\"Empathy is feeling with someone - you can imagine how it is from inside their side. Sympathy is feeling sorry for them, which is kind but stands further away.\"],[\"sympathy\",\"Sympathy is feeling sorry for someone's trouble and saying so: 'you have my sympathy'. Empathy is the one where you feel it with them.\"],[\"apathy\",\"Apathy is not caring when caring would be reasonable - the flat 'what's the point'. It is not the same as being calm or easy-going.\"],[\"ambivalent\",\"Ambivalent means holding both feelings at once: wanting the job and dreading it. It does not mean you do not care.\"],[\"indifferent\",\"Indifferent means it genuinely makes no difference to you either way. Being indifferent is flatter than disliking something.\"],[\"boundaries\",\"A boundary is a limit you set on what you will do or accept: 'I don't answer work messages after eight'. It is a rule for yourself, not a demand on someone else - which is why saying it out loud is the hard part.\"],[\"closure\",\"Closure is the sense of an ending that lets you stop going over something: an explanation, an apology, a last conversation. Plenty of people never get it, and you can still move on without it.\"],[\"narcissist\",\"A narcissist is someone with an inflated sense of their own importance and little room for other people's feelings. The word is used loosely in ordinary talk; as a diagnosis it is a specific personality disorder, and only a professional can make it.\"],[\"validation\",\"Validation is being told that what you feel or did makes sense: 'that's reasonable'. People want it because being disbelieved is a sting of its own.\"],[\"redundancy\",\"Redundancy is losing your job because the role is going, not because of anything you did: the work is cut, moved or merged. It usually means notice plus a statutory payment based on your age and length of service.\"],[\"furlough\",\"Furlough is a temporary lay-off where your employer keeps your job but stops paying you - or the state pays part of your wage through the employer.\"],[\"appraisal\",\"An appraisal is the regular sit-down where your manager goes over your work: what went well, what to change, and sometimes pay. Bring your own list of what you did.\"],[\"paradox\",\"A paradox is a statement that seems to contradict itself but may still be true: 'the more you learn, the less you know'.\"],[\"hypothetical\",\"Hypothetical means imagined rather than real: 'hypothetically, if I left'. People use it to ask a question without owning it.\"],[\"arbitrary\",\"Arbitrary means decided without a rule or reason: an arbitrary deadline, an arbitrary number. It is a criticism of how the choice was made.\"],[\"mitigate\",\"To mitigate is to make something less bad: extra drainage mitigates flooding. Do not confuse it with 'militate', which means to weigh against.\"],[\"inertia\",\"Inertia is the tendency to carry on as you were: in physics, an object's resistance to a change of movement; at home, the reason a routine outlasts its point.\"],[\"reiterate\",\"To reiterate is to say something again, usually to be sure it was heard: 'let me reiterate the deadline'.\"],[\"satire\",\"Satire uses humour to mock something in order to change it: a sketch that makes a habit ridiculous. It is aimed at the powerful, which is what separates it from a joke at someone's expense.\"],[\"sarcasm\",\"Sarcasm is saying the opposite of what you mean with a bite to it: 'great, another meeting'. Irony is the wider pattern; sarcasm is aimed at a person.\"],[\"stereotype\",\"A stereotype is a fixed, over-simple picture of a group, applied to individuals who may not fit it at all.\"],[\"prejudice\",\"Prejudice is judging before you know: a set view about a group of people, held regardless of what they are actually like.\"],[\"discrimination\",\"Discrimination is treating someone worse because of who they are - their sex, race, age, disability, religion and so on - rather than what they do.\"],[\"consent\",\"Consent is a clear yes, given freely, and it can be taken back at any point. Someone asleep, too drunk to follow what is happening, or being pressured cannot give it.\"],[\"placebo\",\"A placebo is a treatment with no active ingredient in it. People often improve anyway, which is the placebo effect, and it is why trials compare against one.\"],[\"kudos\",\"Kudos is praise and credit for something done: 'kudos for getting through that'. It is uncountable - you get kudos, not a kudo.\"],[\"schadenfreude\",\"Schadenfreude is pleasure at someone else's bad luck - German for 'harm-joy', and borrowed because it names the feeling exactly.\"],[\"zeitgeist\",\"Zeitgeist is the mood of a period: the ideas and tastes that feel typical of the time. German for 'time-spirit'.\"]]}")?;
