let __v_3483 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$dict\":[[\"noun\",\"A noun names a person, place or thing - Jess, Leeds, table, kindness. If you can put 'the' in front of it, it is a noun.\"],[\"verb\",\"A verb is the doing or being word: run, eat, seems, is. Every sentence needs one, and the verb changes with the time - I walk today, I walked yesterday.\"],[\"adjective\",\"An adjective describes a noun: a red bus, a tired dog, an honest answer. It sits before the noun or after 'is' - the bus is red.\"],[\"adverb\",\"An adverb says how, when or where something happened: she sang loudly, we left early, he waited outside. Many end in -ly, but not all do (soon, well, fast).\"],[\"pronoun\",\"A pronoun stands in for a noun so you are not repeating it: I, you, he, she, it, we, they, me, him, her, them. 'Sam lost Sam's keys' is why we have them.\"],[\"preposition\",\"A preposition says where or when in relation to something: in, on, under, between, before, after, with. The cat sat on the mat - 'on' is the preposition.\"],[\"conjunction\",\"A conjunction joins words or clauses: and, but, because, although, so. 'I was late because the bus broke down' joins two ideas with 'because'.\"],[\"tense\",\"Tense is when the verb happens: past (I cooked), present (I cook, I am cooking), future (I will cook). Pick one and keep to it within a sentence.\"],[\"clause\",\"A clause is a group of words with its own verb and subject: 'when the bus came' and 'we got on'. A sentence is one or more clauses; a phrase has no verb at all.\"],[\"sentence\",\"A sentence needs a subject and a verb and makes one complete point, starting with a capital and ending with a full stop: 'The bus was late.'\"],[\"fronted adverbial\",\"A fronted adverbial is the phrase that opens a sentence and says when, where or how: 'After lunch, we walked.' 'Slowly, the door opened.' Put a comma after it, and make sure the thing it describes is the subject of the sentence - 'Walking home, the rain started' says the rain was walking.\"],[\"split infinitive\",\"A split infinitive puts a word between to and the verb: 'to boldly go'. It is not an error, whatever you were taught - split it when it reads better and move the word when it gets clumsy: 'to go boldly'.\"],[\"dangling modifier\",\"A dangling modifier is an opening phrase that describes the wrong thing: 'Walking to work, the rain soaked me' says the rain was walking. Name the person straight after the comma - 'Walking to work, I was soaked by the rain' - or turn the phrase into a clause.\"],[\"whom\",\"Use whom after a preposition and where it is the one being acted on: 'to whom it may concern', 'the person whom I met'. If you can say he or she, write who; if you would say him or her, write whom. In speech who nearly always sounds right.\"],[\"adverbial\",\"An adverbial is any phrase that says when, where, how or why: 'at six', 'in the kitchen', 'very slowly', 'because it rained'. It is not always an adverb - it can be a whole phrase or clause.\"],[\"apostrophe\",\"An apostrophe does two jobs: it marks something missing (do not becomes don't) or shows belonging (Jess's coat). It never makes a plain plural - two cats, not two cat's.\"],[\"hashtag\",\"A hashtag is the # sign used as a label: #slowcooking groups posts about one thing. On a UK keyboard it is Shift and 3, and on a phone it is on the symbols page.\"],[\"number sign\",\"The number sign is # - Shift and 3 on a UK keyboard. In the US it is how you write a number (No. 5) and online it starts a hashtag.\"],[\"ampersand\",\"An ampersand is the & sign and it means 'and'. Write and out in a sentence; the sign is for names and headings, like Marks & Spencer or R&D.\"],[\"at sign\",\"The at sign is @ - Shift and 2 on a UK keyboard - and it is in every email address: name@example.com.\"],[\"asterisk\",\"An asterisk is the * sign, a small star. It marks a footnote or shows anything goes (*), and on a phone keyboard it is usually behind the ?123 key.\"],[\"slash\",\"A slash is the / sign, and it mostly means 'or' (and/or) or separates the parts of a date (12/05/2024). A backslash \\\\ is the other way over and turns up in computer paths rather than in writing.\"],[\"backslash\",\"A backslash is the \\\\ sign, the mirror of a slash. It almost never belongs in writing - it separates folders in a Windows file path, like C:\\\\Users\\\\me.\"],[\"underscore\",\"An underscore is the _ sign, the low line under a letter. It joins words in file names and usernames (my_notes.txt); a hyphen is the shorter mark at half height.\"],[\"tilde\",\"A tilde is the ~ sign, the small wavy line. It means 'about' (~20 people came), and on most keyboards it is Shift and the hash key.\"],[\"pipe\",\"A pipe is the | sign, the upright bar. Most people call it the pipe or the vertical bar, and it separates options in a computer command; in writing a slash does the job better.\"],[\"caret\",\"A caret is the ^ sign, a small wedge above the line. In proofreading it marks where a word is to be slipped in, and in maths it means to the power of - 2^3 is eight.\"],[\"bullet point\",\"A bullet point is the dot that starts each item in a list. Keep every item to a line, start each one the same way, and leave the full stops off short items.\"],[\"curly bracket\",\"Curly brackets { } group things in maths and code, and square brackets [ ] hold your own words inside someone else's quotation. Round brackets ( ) take an aside the sentence could do without.\"],[\"square bracket\",\"Square brackets [ ] hold your own words inside a quotation, and they mark a missing word in a transcript: 'she said it was [unclear] on the tape'.\"],[\"angle bracket\",\"Angle brackets < > mark a tag in code and stand in for a word to fill in later, as in <your name>. The plain brackets ( ) are the ones for writing.\"]]}")?;
